  "query_preview": "..Scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue,\nmainly across central Utah, before diminishing after midnight.\nThese showers were aided by a slight increase in moisture today, \nas PWATs increased to between 1-1.2 inches across much of the \nforecast area. For Tuesday, expect drier air to spread into the \nsouthwestern quadrant of Utah, while a pool of moisture lingers \nover northern Utah. This will result in scattered convection \nacross northern Utah Tuesday afternoon and evening, and very \nisolated convective activity across southern Utah. This idea is \nwell supported by various CAMs. \n\nAs high pressure over New Mexico amplifies Tuesday night, a brief\nperiod of southerly flow will spread some moisture back into the\narea. This will bring an uptick in convective activity for\nWednesday. However, this should be the last gasp for the monsoonal\npattern for a little while, as the high pressure will then start\nto slowly shift westward across Arizona through the weekend. This\nwill spread a drier westerly flow into the area beginning\nThursday and continuing through the weekend and at least the first\nhalf of next week. \n\nTemperatures will continue to trend slightly cooler midweek with\nthe moisture. However, as the airmass dries out and strong high \npressure shifts over the area late week and over the weekend,\nexpect a very warm airmass to settle into the area. Global models\nshow H7 temperatures ranging from 20-22C across the forecast area\nwith pockets of up to 23C. Most valleys can expect to see high\ntemperatures of 100 degrees or higher once again (over 110 degrees\nin Lower Washington County and the Glen Canyon area). The ECMWF \nExtreme Forecast Index is showing a strong signal for anomalously \nhigh temperatures during this time. Latest HeatRisk is Major to \nExtreme for most valleys, especially over the weekend. Models \nindicate a bit of a cooling trend early next week as the ridge \nflattens in response to a series of shortwave troughs rippling \neast near the Canadian border.",
